  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "OH/8VQfgsK/87atj62Q5mRdySHI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:54.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Sync between two reactors is asymmetric, and `ChannelScheme` is how you declare which end you are:\n\n```typescript\nenum ChannelScheme {\n  CONNECT = \"connect\",\n  SWITCHBOARD = \"switchboard\",\n}\n```\n\n`ChannelScheme.CONNECT` selects `GqlRequestChannelFactory` \u2014 the client side, which polls for inbound operations and pushes its own outbound ones. `ChannelScheme.SWITCHBOARD` selects `GqlResponseChannelFactory` \u2014 the server side, which is push-driven and does not poll.\n\nThe two factories have different constructor shapes, and the reason is instructive: `GqlRequestChannelFactory` takes `(logger, jwtHandler, queue)` while `GqlResponseChannelFactory` takes only `(logger)`. The request side needs the queue because polling applies **backpressure** \u2014 a tick defers when the reactor queue depth exceeds `maxQueueDepth`. The response side has no timer to throttle, so it needs neither.\n\nTwo consequences follow from the asymmetry:\n\n- `withJwtHandler` only flows through the `CONNECT` factory. On the `SWITCHBOARD` path it is accepted and **ignored** \u2014 the server does not authenticate outward, it authenticates inbound callers. The handler signature is `(url: string) => Promise<string | undefined>`, called per request with the target URL.\n- `ISyncManager.triggerPull(name)` on a `GqlResponseChannel` is a **no-op**, because there is nothing to pull; that channel is push-driven by design.\n\nToday GraphQL over HTTP is the only shipped transport, and the channel config `type` string is `\"gql\"` for both sides. `GqlResponseChannel` and its factory are Switchboard-side primitives relevant to `reactor-api` wiring rather than typical app code \u2014 application developers pick `CONNECT` almost every time.\n\nThe docs flag one instability here: `IChannelFactory.instance`'s `cursorStorage` and `operationIndex` parameters are marked for removal in source, so do not depend on the factory signature staying fixed.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/04-Synchronization.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:54.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:54.000Z"
    }
  },
